WHAT WAS TAKEN. Thirty-five icons, in two files.

`index.html`, the landing page's scene « Choisissez une vue. »
(src/vitrine/stage.js), draws them in its tab bar, its pause control and its
door to the globe:

    plane           — the « Avions » tab,
    house           — the « Immobilier » tab,
    car             — the « Trafic » tab,
    zap             — the « Énergie » tab,
    bus-front       — the « Bus » tab,
    radio-tower     — the « Numérique » tab,
    pause, play     — « Mettre en pause » / « Reprendre »,
    arrow-up-right  — « Ouvrir dans le globe ».

`arrow-up-right` is one of the icons Lucide derived from Feather, so the MIT
notice covers it; the eight others are Lucide's own, under ISC.

`src/data/lucideIcons.js` draws four more (retrieved 2026-09-22 at the same
commit) as the member tiles of the « Infrastructure numérique » row in the map
key:

    cable        — the « Câbles » tile (submarine cables),
    database     — the « Data centers » tile,
    radio-tower  — the « Antennes » tile,
    signal-high  — the « Couverture 4G » tile.

`database` is one of the icons Lucide derived from Feather, so the MIT notice
covers it; `cable`, `radio-tower` and `signal-high` are Lucide's own, under
ISC.

The same file draws nine more (retrieved 2026-09-23 at the same commit) in
the Layers panel: one glyph per group in its category rail, and the controls
of the list that opens beside it (`src/data/layerPanelRail.js`):

    plane           — « Ciel & mer »,
    building        — « Bâti & territoire »,
    car             — « Mobilité terrestre »,
    zap             — « Énergie »,
    triangle-alert  — « Risques & environnement »,
    plug            — « Jeux branchés »,
    search          — the rail's search entry and the list's search field,
    pin             — « Garder la liste ouverte »,
    x               — « Fermer la liste ».

`search` and `x` are icons Lucide derived from Feather, so the MIT notice
covers them; the seven others are Lucide's own, under ISC. « Réseaux &
capteurs » reuses `radio-tower`, listed above. There the
child elements are copied verbatim into an `<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24">` that
carries Lucide's own stroke attributes (width 2), and the result is used as a
CSS mask, so the tile's colour paints through the strokes.

And six more (retrieved 2026-09-23 at the same commit) in the globe's top
bar, its place search and its Appearance panel (`src/globeShell.js`):

    sliders-horizontal — « Apparence »,
    ellipsis           — « Plus d'actions »,
    clock              — a recent place in the search menu,
    map-pin            — a city in the same menu,
    check              — the chosen map style,
    chevron-right      — « Réglages avancés ».

`ellipsis` (Feather's `more-horizontal`), `clock`, `check` and
`chevron-right` are icons Lucide derived from Feather, so the MIT notice
covers them; `sliders-horizontal` and `map-pin` are Lucide's own, under ISC.
They are drawn exactly as the Layers panel's are: copied verbatim, used as a
CSS mask.

And five more (retrieved 2026-09-23 at the same commit) in the globe's
navigation bar at the bottom of the screen (`src/globeNav.js`):

    chevron-left — « Vue précédente »,
    scan         — « Vue du dessus »,
    minus        — « Dézoomer »,
    plus         — « Zoomer »,
    rotate-ccw   — « Réinitialiser ».

`chevron-left`, `minus`, `plus` and `rotate-ccw` are icons Lucide derived
from Feather, so the MIT notice covers them; `scan` is Lucide's own, under
ISC. They are drawn exactly as the top bar's are: copied verbatim, used as a
CSS mask.

And two more (retrieved 2026-09-23 at the same commit) as the member tiles of
the « Incendies » row in the map key (`src/data/lucideIcons.js`):

    flame            — « Détections récentes »,
    rotate-ccw-clock — « Grands incendies » (the icon Lucide used to call
                       `history`).

Both are Lucide's own, under ISC, and are drawn exactly as the other tiles'
are: copied verbatim, used as a CSS mask.

And one more (retrieved 2026-09-24 at the same commit) as a tile under the
« Aéroports » row of the Layers panel (`src/data/lucideIcons.js`):

    audio-lines — « Bruit & urbanisme ».

It is Lucide's own, under ISC. The row's other tile, « Les aéroports », and
its airport menu reuse `plane`, listed above. Drawn exactly as the other
tiles' are: copied verbatim, used as a CSS mask. And `style.css` draws
`arrow-up-right`, listed above, on the key's « Consulter le plan officiel »
button, inline as a CSS mask. (The noise layer's airport tags on the globe
draw Maki's `airport`, not this `plane`: see `licenses/maki/NOTICE`.)

And two more (retrieved 2026-09-24 at the same commit) as the mode tiles
under the « Zone de chalandise » row of the Layers panel
(`src/data/lucideIcons.js`):

    footprints — « À pied »,
    bike       — « Vélo ».

Both are Lucide's own, under ISC. The row's third tile, « Voiture », reuses
`car`, listed above. Drawn exactly as the other tiles' are: copied verbatim,
used as a CSS mask. `style.css` draws `map-pin`, listed above, on the row's
« Changer le point » button, inline as a CSS mask. (The point the catchment
is measured from is drawn on the globe, and beside its address in the panel,
as a filled pin of this app's own, not as Lucide's `map-pin`.)

They are INTERFACE icons, drawn at 18-22 CSS px in a menu bar, which is the job
Lucide authors for; nothing here is drawn on the globe, where the cartographic
sets (Maki, Temaki) keep that role. They were chosen because the approved mock
of the scene (2026-09-21) was drawn with exactly these six category glyphs.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. No coordinate.

Each icon's child elements (`<path>`, `<circle>`, `<rect>`) are copied
verbatim into an inline `<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24">`, inside one `<g>` that
sets the stroke. Lucide publishes a stroke width of 2; the page draws 1.8, the
width of the arrows it already had, so the new glyphs sit at the same weight.
That is a presentation attribute on the group, not an edit of the artwork.
